course: dictionary
lesson: 6368
title: Muck: many meanings, one word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the context tells you which one you need. «Take off your boots, they're covered in muck!» Here muck means dirt, mud or something wet and dirty, because boots get covered in mud, not in poo.
explain: On a farm, muck can mean animal dung, which is poo. «In winter the cows live in big sheds, and the muck from the sheds goes back on the fields in spring.» The cows and the fields are the clues: this muck is animal waste that farmers spread on the land as fertiliser.
explain: Muck can also be part of a phrase. «Stop mucking about!» Mucking about means behaving in a silly way and wasting time. This is an informal, everyday phrase, so you will hear it at home and at school more than in formal writing.
explain: To check your meaning, ask: what word would fit here? Put your meaning into the sentence. If «dirt» fits the boots sentence and «behaving silly» fits the mucking about sentence, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Muck is an informal, everyday word.
fact: «Muck» means dirt, mud or something wet and dirty.
fact: «Muck» can also mean animal dung, often spread on fields as manure.
fact: «Mucking about» (UK) means behaving in a silly way and wasting time.
fact: «Muck out» means to clean an animal's stable; «muck in» means to help with the work.
exercise: «Take off your boots, they're covered in muck!» What does muck mean here? | *Dirt, mud or something wet and dirty | A kind of farm animal | A silly way to waste time | Animal dung spread on a field
exercise: «In winter the cows live in big sheds, and the muck from the sheds goes back on the fields in spring.» What is the muck in this sentence? | Hay for the cows to eat | A new kind of tractor | *Animal dung, spread on fields as fertiliser | Rain water running off the roof
exercise: «Stop mucking about!» What does mucking about mean? | Helping the farmer with the work | Spoiling a picture on purpose | *Behaving in a silly way and wasting time | Cleaning out a stable
test: «Everyone must muck in and help clean the yard.» What does muck in mean here? | To wander off and play | *To help with the work | To spoil the work | To cover things in dirt
test: «The farmer will muck out the stable before lunch.» What does muck out mean? | To spoil something | To play in the mud | To feed the horses | *To clean an animal's stable
exam: «Farmers spread slurry, a runny mix of muck and water, on the fields.» What is slurry? | Dry hay for the cows | A kind of fish | Clean water from a tap | *A runny mix of muck and water
